1:["$","div",null,{"className":"blog-shell","children":[["$","header",null,{"className":"blog-header","children":[["$","a",null,{"href":"/","className":"wordmark","aria-label":"Wen Reloz, home","children":["wr",["$","span",null,{"children":"."}]]}],["$","nav",null,{"aria-label":"Primary navigation","children":[["$","a",null,{"href":"/","children":"PORTFOLIO"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"/"}],["$","a",null,{"href":"/blog","children":"BLOG"}]]}]]}],["$","main",null,{"className":"article-shell","children":[["$","a",null,{"href":"/blog","className":"back-link","children":[["$","$Ld",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":[["path",{"d":"m12 19-7-7 7-7","key":"1l729n"}],["path",{"d":"M19 12H5","key":"x3x0zl"}]],"className":"lucide-arrow-left","size":15}]," All articles"]}],["$","article",null,{"children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"eyebrow","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"short-rule"}]," ","C# / ASYNCHRONY"]}],["$","h1",null,{"children":["Async/Await Is Not a New Thread",["$","span",null,{"children":"."}]]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"article-lede","children":"The mental model that makes asynchronous C# easier to write, debug, and explain."}],"$undefined",["$","div",null,{"className":"article-rule"}],[["$","div","Async and await may be the most deceptively simple features in C#. The code reads from top to bottom, yet the method can pause, return control to its caller, and continue later. That makes asynchronous code approachable—but it also creates a common misconception: async does not mean “run this on a new thread.”",{"children":[["$","p",null,{"children":"Async and await may be the most deceptively simple features in C#. The code reads from top to bottom, yet the method can pause, return control to its caller, and continue later. That makes asynchronous code approachable—but it also creates a common misconception: async does not mean “run this on a new thread.”"}],false]}],["$","div","An async method begins running synchronously on the current thread. When it reaches an await whose operation is not complete, the method suspends. Control returns to the caller, and the remainder of the method is registered as a continuation. When the operation finishes, that continuation can resume. The current thread was not forced to sit idle, and async itself did not create another one.",{"children":[["$","p",null,{"children":"An async method begins running synchronously on the current thread. When it reaches an await whose operation is not complete, the method suspends. Control returns to the caller, and the remainder of the method is registered as a continuation. When the operation finishes, that continuation can resume. The current thread was not forced to sit idle, and async itself did not create another one."}],false]}],["$","div","This is especially valuable for I/O-bound work. While an application waits for a database query, HTTP response, or file operation, a thread does not need to be occupied simply waiting. In an ASP.NET Core service, that means available threads can serve other requests, which helps the application stay responsive under load.",{"children":[["$","p",null,{"children":"This is especially valuable for I/O-bound work. While an application waits for a database query, HTTP response, or file operation, a thread does not need to be occupied simply waiting. In an ASP.NET Core service, that means available threads can serve other requests, which helps the application stay responsive under load."}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"code-block","aria-label":"C# example","children":["$","code",null,{"children":"public async Task<Patient?> GetPatientAsync(int id, CancellationToken token)\n{\n    return await db.Patients\n        .AsNoTracking()\n        .SingleOrDefaultAsync(x => x.Id == id, token);\n}"}]}]]}],["$","div","CPU-bound work is different. A large calculation still needs processor time. If it must leave the current thread, Task.Run can schedule that work on the thread pool. The distinction matters: await coordinates asynchronous operations; it does not magically make expensive computation free.",{"children":["$Le",false]}],"$Lf","$L10"],"$L11"]}],"$L12"]}],"$L13"]}]
e:["$","p",null,{"children":"CPU-bound work is different. A large calculation still needs processor time. If it must leave the current thread, Task.Run can schedule that work on the thread pool. The distinction matters: await coordinates asynchronous operations; it does not magically make expensive computation free."}]
f:["$","div","The practical habits follow from this model. Return Task instead of async void except for event handlers. Pass cancellation tokens through the call chain. Await work instead of blocking with .Result or .Wait(). When independent operations can run together, start them first and coordinate them with Task.WhenAll.",{"children":[["$","p",null,{"children":"The practical habits follow from this model. Return Task instead of async void except for event handlers. Pass cancellation tokens through the call chain. Await work instead of blocking with .Result or .Wait(). When independent operations can run together, start them first and coordinate them with Task.WhenAll."}],false]}]
10:["$","div","Understanding the mechanism changes async code from syntax you memorize into behavior you can reason about. When a request stalls or a continuation runs somewhere unexpected, the right question is not “which new thread did async create?” It is “what operation are we waiting for, and where will its continuation run?”",{"children":[["$","p",null,{"children":"Understanding the mechanism changes async code from syntax you memorize into behavior you can reason about. When a request stalls or a continuation runs somewhere unexpected, the right question is not “which new thread did async create?” It is “what operation are we waiting for, and where will its continuation run?”"}],false]}]
